  "dom_id": "step-q163775",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)\u22481+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{\u2033}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ what type of point is $a?$",
  "worked_solution": "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{\u2032}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{\u2033}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{\u2033}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{\u2033}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)\u2248f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{\u2033}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{\u2032}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{\u2033}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-3<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-9<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$",
